== Ancient Macedonian Contribution

As a consequence of the [[ Macedonians | ancient-macedonians ]]' role in the
formation of Koine Greek,
[[ Ancient Macedonian | ancient-macedonian-language ]] contributed
considerable elements to the language, including some military terminology
(διμοιρίτης, ταξίαρχος, ὑπασπισταί, etc.). Among the many contributions were
the general use of the [[ first declension ]] grammar for male and female
nouns with an -as ending, attested in the [[ genitive | genitive-case ]] of
Macedonian coinage from the early 4th century BC of
[[ Amyntas III | amyntas-iii-of-macedon ]] (ΑΜΥΝΤΑ in the genitive; the Attic
form that fell into disuse would be ΑΜΥΝΤΟΥ). There were changes in verb
conjugation such as in the [[ imperative | imperative-mood ]] δέξα //dexa//
attested in Macedonian sling stones found in Asiatic battlefields, that became
adopted in place of the Attic forms. Koine Greek established a
[[ spirantisation ]] of beta, gamma, and delta, which has been attributed to
the Macedonian influence.[[ ^34 ]] The term "Macedonian" ended up meaning the
Koine Greek in classical sources.[[ ^35 ]]

== Sample Koine texts

The following texts show differences from Attic Greek in all aspects –
grammar, morphology, vocabulary and can be inferred to show differences in
phonology.

The following comments illustrate the phonological development within the
period of Koine. The phonetic transcriptions are tentative and are intended to
illustrate two different stages in the reconstructed development, an early
conservative variety still relatively close to Classical Attic, and a somewhat
later, more progressive variety approaching Modern Greek in some respects.

=== Sample 1 – A Roman decree

The following excerpt, from a decree of the Roman Senate to the town of
[[ Thisbae ]] in [[ Boeotia ]] in 170 BC, is rendered in a reconstructed
pronunciation representing a hypothetical conservative variety of mainland
Greek Koiné in the early Roman period.[[ ^36 ]] The transcription shows
raising of η to , partial (pre-consonantal/word-final) raising of ῃ and ει to
, retention of pitch accent, and retention of word-initial (the
[[ rough breathing ]]).

